The one judgment call worth flagging: [38;5;10mhelperBad()[0m is a CWE-562 defect — it returns the address of an automatic array. I did not fix it, but "reproduce it exactly" needs a definition of [3mexactly[23m, since the C is undefined behavior. I resolved it empirically: GCC diagnoses [38;5;10m-Wreturn-local-addr[0m and emits a literal null return ([38;5;10mmov $0x0,%eax[0m) at every optimization level I checked ([38;5;10m-O0[0m, [38;5;10m-O1[0m, [38;5;10m-O2[0m, [38;5;10m-O3[0m, [38;5;10m-Os[0m), so the compiled library's observable behavior is [38;5;10mprintLine(NULL)[0m, which prints nothing. The Rust returns [38;5;10mptr::null_mut()[0m to match, with the reasoning documented at the call site. Writing the literal dangling-pointer return in Rust would have been UB there too and would [3mnot[23m have reproduced the empty output. Note this pins the behavior to GCC's substitution — a compiler that instead returned the live stack address would print garbage, and no Rust translation could track that.[0m[0m
